{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that a 'Perturbation Response' is the ONLY evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability from mere checklist compliance is false. The problem asks to identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere compliance; an answer must survive this scope. A different evidence type that also distinguishes the two within a single evaluation forms a counterexample: repeated same-outcome trials with novel, semantically equivalent cases. A compliant panel tracking the correct answer merely by surface-level checklist cues will fail when the surface cues are changed but the objective outcome is held constant, whereas a genuinely reliable panel's judgment remains stable across semantically equivalent perturbations. This 'invariance to paraphrase' evidence is entirely distinct from the 'exception report' the target describes, yet it fully distinguishes reliability from compliance within a single evaluation. The target's own counterconditions do not protect the exclusivity claim against this alternative, because the alternative does not depend on the panel flagging an item as not applicable or misleading; it depends on stability across rephrased cases. Because the target says 'only' and a different single-evaluation distinguisher exists, the claim is too strong and fails the problem's scope.","counterexample":[{"differs_from":"perturbation-response/exception-report","distinguisher":"invariance-to-paraphrase","evidence":"Within one evaluation, re-run cases with the same objective outcome but rephrased so surface checklist cues are altered. A merely compliant panel will produce inconsistent judgments because the surface cues changed, while a genuinely reliable panel produces stable judgments.","rejects_exclusivity":true}],"target":"e3a69bf8a5313f63b0d7616f39053d6f58d700ff1acb2d671432056915f9e2a0"}]}